{k} \\\\  & = ( 0 \\cdot  2 - ( - 3 ) \\cdot  ( - 1 ) ) \\mathbf{i} - ( 4 \\cdot  2 - ( - 3 ) \\cdot  5 ) \\mathbf{j} + ( 4 \\cdot  ( - 1 ) - 0 \\cdot  5 ) \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = - 3 \\mathbf{i} - 23 \\mathbf{j} - 4 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  - 3 , - 23 , - 4 \\rangle  .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\times  \\mathbf{b} \\, | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{( - 3 {)}^{2} + ( - 23 {)}^{2} + ( - 4 {)}^{2}} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{554} .\\end{array}$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}\n$$" 13194139534719 false] [17592186105514 1100 "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$, if $A(1,0,-2)$, $B(-2,0,1)$, $C(1,1,-1)$." 13194139534719 false] [17592186105514 1100 "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ if $A(1,0,-2),$ $B(-2,0,1),$ $C(1,1,-1).$" 13194139534719 true] [17592186105514 1145 "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( - 2 \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{k} ) - ( \\mathbf{i} - 2 \\mathbf{k} ) \\\\  & = - 3 \\mathbf{i} + 3 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  - 3 , 0 , 3 \\rangle \\\\ \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k} ) - ( \\mathbf{i} - 2 \\mathbf{k} ) \\\\  & = \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  0 , 1 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ - 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{i} - \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}- 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{j} + \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}- 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = ( 0 \\cdot  1 - 3 \\cdot  1 ) \\mathbf{i} - ( - 3 \\cdot  1 - 3 \\cdot  0 ) \\mathbf{j} + ( - 3 \\cdot  1 - 0 \\cdot  0 ) \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = - 3 \\mathbf{i} + 3 \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  - 3 , 3 , - 3 \\rangle  .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A & = | \\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{( - 3 {)}^{2} + {3}^{2} + ( - 3 {)}^{2}} \\\\  & = 3 \\sqrt{3} .\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$" 13194139534719 false] [17592186105514 1145 "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i+k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+j-k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1)i-(-3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0)j+(-3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534719 true] [17592186105515 1100 "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ if $A(-1,1,0)$, $B(1,1,2)$, $C(1,-1,1)$." 13194139534719 false] [17592186105515 1100 "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ if $A(-1,1,0),$ $B(1,1,2),$ $C(1,-1,1).$" 13194139534719 true] [17592186105515 1145 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH$, we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}$. To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2 \\mathbf{k} ) - ( - \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} ) \\\\  & = 2 \\mathbf{i} + 2 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  2 , 0 , 2 \\rangle \\\\ \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} ) - ( - \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} ) \\\\  & = 2 \\mathbf{i} - 2 \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  2 , - 2 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & - 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 2 \\\\ - 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{i} - \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{j} + \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & - 2\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = ( 0 \\cdot  1 - 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) ) \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 \\cdot  1 - 2 \\cdot  2 ) \\mathbf{j} + ( 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) - 0 \\cdot  2 ) \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = 4 \\mathbf{i} + 2 \\mathbf{j} - 4 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  4 , 2 , - 4 \\rangle  .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A & = | \\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{{4}^{2} + {2}^{2} + ( - 4 {)}^{2}} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{36} \\\\  & = 6.\\end{array}$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A B & = | \\overrightarrow{AB} | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{{2}^{2} + {0}^{2} + {2}^{2}} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{8} \\\\  & = 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, using the formula\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{△} &= \\frac{1}{2}CH \\cdot AB \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{2}CH \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n3 &= CH \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\nCH &= \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nCH &= \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534719 false] [17592186105515 1145 "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =4i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534719 true] [17592186105656 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305332 13194139534719 true] [17592186105656 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305339 13194139534719 true] [17592186105656 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305346 13194139534719 true] [17592186105656 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305349 13194139534719 true] [17592186105656 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305357 13194139534719 true] [17592186105657 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305311 13194139534719 true] [17592186105657 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305318 13194139534719 true] [17592186105657 1020 #edb/ref 17592186305361 13194139534719 true] [17592186105657 1020 #edb/ref 175